. Prophase (The "Packing and Moving" Stage)
Duration: The longest stage of mitosis.
The Main Goals: Condense the DNA and build the "pulling machinery."

Chromatin Condensation (The "Suitcase Packing"): Throughout Interphase, DNA exists as loose, tangled chromatin, which is useless for traveling. During Prophase, enzymes called condensins coil the chromatin tightly into discrete, highly compact, rod-like chromosomes. Under a microscope, you can clearly see that each chromosome is shaped like an "X" because it consists of two identical sister chromatids, glued together along their entire length by protein complexes called cohesins. They are joined at a narrow, pinched region called the centromere.

Nucleolus Disappears: The nucleolus (the dense region where ribosomes are made) dissolves and disperses because it is no longer needed for the moment.

Centrosome Migration (The "Tug-of-War Begins"): In animal cells, the centrosome (the microtubule-organizing center) contains a pair of centrioles. This centrosome duplicated in G2, and now the two new centrosomes begin moving apart. They are pushed in opposite directions by growing microtubules that elongate and push against each other. By the end of Prophase, they have reached opposite poles (ends) of the cell.

Spindle Formation: As the centrosomes move, they radiate a star-shaped array of microtubules called asters, along with the beginnings of the mitotic spindle. These spindle fibers are made of tubulin protein and will eventually act like fishing lines to catch the chromosomes.

Nuclear Envelope Breakdown Begins: The nuclear membrane starts to destabilize. Proteins called nuclear pore complexes are phosphorylated (tagged with a phosphate group), signaling that the envelope is about to be dismantled.
